## Findings
- Curve network: a cube collapsed to one vertex, extruded along the blueprint lines with 4 edges and 5 points (154 to 191 s); the main hood and fender line gets 12 to 13 points plus 4 to 5 tight points at the nose curl (228 s); Space then Relax (249 to 268 s); each line fitted side, top (shear about the nose vertex), front (271 to 300 s); the outer line raised from 5 to 13 edges to match the inner (333 s); the rear end from 1 to 4 edges (352 s). Lines meet only at patch corners.
- Fill: default Grid Fill twists (397 s); Span 4, Offset 8 gives a clean 13 x 4 (400 s). The fender patch shares the hood's 13 edge side, 13 x 4 (443, 490 s). Corner rebuilt as 1 triangle + 2 quads, the triangle split by a loop cut (503 to 505 s). Lower strip: far edge dissolved from 13 to 9, filled 9 x 2 with Span 2 (536 to 542 s).
- Wheel arches: not built in the video; panels (fender, bumper, hood) are separate objects (615 to 630 s).
- Crease: an existing 9 edge row beveled, offset 3.45 mm, 2 segments, profile 1.0, loop slide on, miters sharp (566 s); the fan at the bevel end (valence about 6) removed by slide, merge, dissolve, the support spreading over about 2 cells (571 to 596 s); 96 faces, all quads.
- Poles only at patch corners; valence 3 and 5 pair from the split triangle; no n-gons (triangles = 2 x faces).
- Density: bands of 13 along by 4 across, quads about 1 : 1.3 in plan, about 10 to 12 cm per cage quad (estimate); density rises only at crease supports and the nose curl; mirror X (merge 0.001 m) then Catmull-Clark level 2 (96 to 1536 faces); whole car 28 objects, 443,012 faces at level 2 (615 s).
- Checks: three orthographic views in turn, statistics overlay before each fill, solid shading for dents and pinches, object mode for the subdivided result, real photos beside the viewport, glossy red material for reflections.

## Rules for a procedural car generator
1. Build from closed 4 sided patches; patches meet only at their corners (386 s).
2. Check edge counts before filling: opposite sides must match, else subdivide or dissolve (345 to 362 s).
3. Fill each patch as an n x m Coons grid; vertices (n+1)(m+1), edges n(m+1) + m(n+1), faces nm; a twisted fill fails (400 s vs 397 s).
4. Front quarter of a sports car: hood 13 x 4, fender 13 x 4 sharing the 13 edge side, lower strip 9 x 2; about 10 to 12 cm per cage quad, about 1 : 1.3 (400, 490, 542, 448 s).
5. Neighbouring patches share edge rows with the same count; no T junctions (443, 490 s).
6. Change resolution only by dissolving whole rows where a band narrows (13 to 9, 527 to 542 s).
7. Space points evenly by arc length then relax; along a line max segment / min segment 1.3 or less, except at intended tight radii (249 to 268, 365, 535 s).
8. Pack points only where the profile turns sharply; spacing follows the local radius of curvature (228 s).
9. Fit each curve side, then top outline, then front section (194 to 300 s).
10. 4 to 5 control points per line while shaping; final resolution only once the shape fits (2:53, 3:15).
11. No triangles or n-gons in the delivered cage; a 3 patch corner triangle is split at once into a valence 3 and valence 5 pair (503 to 505 s).
12. Poles only at patch corners, interior valence 4 (530 s).
13. Crease from an existing edge row: 2 support rows at about 0.2 % of the panel length (about 1/30 of the grid spacing) each side, only where the crease shows (566 s, 3.45 mm, 2 segments).
14. A crease ending inside a panel fades over about 2 cells; no vertex above valence 5 at the end (571 to 596 s).
15. Model one half, mirror on X (merge 0.001 m), then subdivide (0 s, 466 s).
16. Judge smoothness on the Catmull-Clark level 2 surface (606, 615 s).
17. Separate panel objects instead of grooves in one shell; openings are open panel edges (615 to 638 s).
18. Validate: wireframe, shaded level 2 for dents, real photos side by side, glossy reflection pass.

Not shown: wheel arch, light housing, grille hole and panel flange topology in a countable wireframe.
